children.translated_text:
Since in the <s>Purāṇa</s> the fame of <s>Kṛṣṇa</s> is that he is 'the lord of <s>Gokula</s>,' that <s>Gokula</s> becomes his abode;
and the condition of dwelling with cows is indeed the basis of the nature of <s>Gokula</s> —
therefore <s>Govardhana</s> here is the foundation of everything. ||1.7||

She bowed at [the queen's] feet — she placed her hand on [<s>Rādhā's</s>] head;
she remained thus on the ground — she raised her and smelled her hair;
she bent her body — she placed [<s>Rādhā</s>] at her side;
with tears she gazed — O, what shall I say of this pair? ||2.5||

'The art of washing the glorious face, hands, and feet; anointing with oil and the like;
bathing, cleansing the body, wearing the two garments; the ritual of <s>ācamana</s> and <s>puṇḍra</s>-marks;
the morning religious observance; divine garments; adornment with rows of jewels;
the arrangement of the flute, horn, peacock-feather, and staff — all this captivates my mind!' ||2.7||
[22] And among those certain body-servants, the special [details] become the subject even of the remaining words of <s>Śeṣa</s> (the infinite serpent). For:

[Verse:] "'May you be victorious in the washing and wiping of [your] feet here!' — such was the prayer;
proper fanning in between, and the well-spoken words of delighted women, laughter-producing;
the serving of delicious [dishes] and the nectar of the grace of <s>Murāri's</s> (Kṛṣṇa's) glance —
where such is the procedure of dining with friends, does it not produce happiness?" [<s>Gopāla-campū</s> 1.2.17, <s>śārdūlavikrīḍita</s>]

 Namely, subsequently, for those in the worlds who will behold Him, the envisioned [form is as follows] —
[93] His face is seen as if presiding over dark lotuses of lustrous smiles;
his pair of eyes, over inner petals of white night-lotuses with delicate, variegated bees;
his nose, over sesame blossoms riveted to blue rain-cloud complexion;
his upper and lower lips, over a gathering of vermillion, mountain-born minerals, <s>javā</s> flowers, <s>bandhūka</s> flowers, and <s>bimba</s> fruits;
his pair of ears, over shoots of dark-hued collyrium-born creepers;
his pair of arms, beautiful at the fingertips, over branches of young <s>tāla</s> palms with fresh sprouts;
his chest bearing the marks named <s>Śrīvatsa</s>, <s>Sindhu</s>, and <s>Vatsa</s>, with the special radiance of a right-spiraling [conch], over cloud-fragments embracing stable lightning. |

 Moreover, by his face he conquers the great lotus;
by his eyes, the lotus;
by his nose, the crocodile [emblem];
by his smile, the jasmine;
by his neck, the conch;
by the tops of his feet, the turtle;
by his luster, the sapphire;
and by all of these together, the smallness of everything.
What more — even <s>Mukunda</s> (liberation) by himself! Thus, it is not impossible that even the treasures — those rare presences occurring at those respective occasions — all converge here simultaneously. |

Nevertheless:
From ten thousand, a hundred thousand becomes a <s>niyuta</s> (million) beginning from thousands of tens;
at twenty <s>lakṣas</s> (two million) <s>Nanda</s> gave that many cows. ||4.17||
Having given twenty <s>lakṣas</s> of cows, with golden horns and associated [ornaments],
his heart remained unfilled, [ready] for giving to yet another. ||4.18||
With ten times ten <s>droṇas</s> (measures), he gave seven mountains of sesame;
the <s>brāhmaṇas</s> considered the weight of the jewels and gold on them to be even greater. ||4.19||
And the gift that was given by him to those deserving of honoraria —
even that, being inexhaustible, led to inexhaustible wonder in others. ||4.20||
Of the innumerable Brahmin wives, there was no counting then;

 But, by the cleverness of his own deeds, he indicates as if to say:
'The very breast is the livelihood of an infant;
you yourself placed it at my mouth;
and if she dies because I drank —
what is my fault? Let it be told by yourself!' // [<s>upajāti</s>]

The extreme sweetness of the enemy of <s>Kaṃsa</s> will not attain the status of provability
even if compounded by tens of millions of ages;
for even that <s>rākṣasī</s>, even though a blood-drinker,
by a mere portion of his touch, attained the finest fragrance! ||5.30||

And there:
He gazes at dear ones, shivers with mucous, constantly moves the pair of his arms,
coos softly and sweetly, and longs for an embrace;
from the gain or loss [of it], this one sparkles, and sometimes even cries;
having drunk the breast-milk, he also sleeps, and again, waking, gives joy. ||6.4||

 Then <s>Tṛṇāvarta</s>, whirling in a vortex of wind upon the celestial path, carrying him aloft there, seized him as if with a noose-grip around his throat. He also battered the cow-settlement with showers of painful hail and the like, bringing it almost to destruction. And there, everything — both the trembling and the fearless — was made to tremble. And then:
Not only outside was everything enveloped by darkness;
the interior of the people too was [enveloped] when <s>Tṛṇāvarta</s> set in motion. // [anuṣṭubh]

Ah! This world is seen as if outside, within the mouth of the child;
then this earth, and upon the earth the region of <s>Mathurā</s>;
therein the clan of <s>Vraja</s>, following which even I hold a boy —
he himself — ah! — how? What indeed? Alas! What is being accomplished? ||7.38|| [<s>pṛthvī</s>]

The mother stealthily advanced to seize her son, the perpetrator of the theft;
seeing [her], he ran off;
this indeed is the well-known [truth] in the world: a hundred
[degrees of vision] belong to the eyes — two for the supporter, [the rest] for the thief! ||8.17|| [upajāti]

 The glorious <s>Kṛṣṇa</s> said:
"By appearance it would be like a bird, but by behavior not bird-like;
is this crane some novice? — A fraudulent stance is observed!" // [<s>Gopāla-campū</s> 1.10.14, <s>anuṣṭubh</s>]

 Thus, having released this one of compact build from his jointless bonds, and following his footpath-trail, he traced the entrance of that hiding-cave. And then:
Immediately breaking open the covering of the cave,
he entered there and manifested a radiance;
from that cave and from those boys on every side,
by his self-revelation, whatever darkness existed — that he removed! ||10.24|| [śālinī]

Of bright and dark luster, they created longing for the beauty of <s>paugaṇḍa</s> (pre-adolescent) grace;
by their mischievousness, they made even the mind of a sage become restless;
having attained the full curriculum of the excellent arts of sweetness in manifold sports,
those two of moon-like faces, extending the nectar of flute-song, sported! ||11.1|| [śārdūlavikrīḍita]

The group of friends says "<s>Kṛṣṇa</s>" and "<s>Rāma</s>" — this [designation],
when analyzed anywhere, proves to be fitting;
for consider that these two also, in the very station of the self,
clearly perform [their deeds] here and there in mutual exchange! // [<s>Gopāla-campū</s> 1.12.10, <s>mālinī</s>]

That is not a forest which is not the auspicious ground of sport;
that is not a sport which does not bear auspicious song;
that is not a song which is not fashioned by the flute;
that is not a flute which does not follow <s>Kṛṣṇa's</s> mouth! // [<s>Gopāla-campū</s> 1.12.11, <s>upajāti</s>]

And those turned back from within the poison-pool
realized even more the nature of that one [<s>Kṛṣṇa</s>];
for, having seen <s>Hari</s> within that [pool],
amidst the fire of <s>kālakūṭa</s> poison,
they considered their own [state to be the same]! // [upajāti]

 In the glorious <s>Bhāgavata</s>, this was indeed brought forth:
'From that time onward, the <s>Vraja</s> of <s>Nanda</s> was endowed with all prosperity;
through the qualities of <s>Hari's</s> abode, it became the pleasure-ground of <s>Ramā</s> (<s>Lakṣmī</s>), O King!' [BhP 10.5.18]

"May the royal couple of the cowherd-settlement attain the status of parents-in-law; may the younger brother of <s>Rāma</s> attain the status of husband;
may this <s>Vṛndāraṇya</s> (Vṛndāvana) always serve as a pleasure-garden for us who always worship;
and these [friends] who, out of affection, with hands mutually clasped, are immersed in you, O <s>Kālindī</s> —
may they, within your waters, attain the status of friends — and in reliance upon you, so do we!" // [<s>Gopāla-campū</s> 1.15.48, <s>śārdūlavikrīḍita</s>]

And then:
All knew <s>Hari's</s> wind-messengers as the life-breath of the world;
but in separation from him, the languid women knew them by the name "swift-goers" [i.e., as omens of death]! // [<s>Gopāla-campū</s> 1.17.38, <s>anuṣṭubh</s>]

"<s>Hari's</s> face, under the pretext of the flute-sound, rains nectar;
the cow's tongue and pair of ears drink this;
the other tongue (of taste) became stunned, motionless regarding its own domain —
"What is this? Is this not that? Is it something like that? Or what?"" // [<s>Gopāla-campū</s> 1.17.46, <s>śikharinī</s>]

"In this forest, the birds are proclaimed as sages;
and <s>Kṛṣṇa</s> is their guru — this we constantly understand;
not otherwise, for whatever is sung by him,
they, stationed all around, listen with a vow of silence!" // [<s>Gopāla-campū</s> 1.17.53, <s>vasantatilakā</s>]

'The one supreme <s>Paramātmā</s> (Supreme Self) is the knower of all fields;
in him the individual souls exist, not excessively different;
the sages who know this are the worshippable among the learned —
therefore, the concealment of these [truths] from them is not fitting!' // [śālinī]

'If this is the view of the <s>yogīs</s>, then let policy too be considered:
the indifferent one is to be shunned like an enemy; the friend is approved as one's own self. // [anuṣṭubh]
If this is the path of <s>dharma</s>, even that is not [established] without deliberation;
for therein, what succeeds for the wise would never succeed for the unwise. // [anuṣṭubh]
<s>Dharma</s> is desired as Vedic alone; the worldly [dharma] is seen in some places;
which of those two is especially approved by you?' // [anuṣṭubh]

'One who of his own accord, by the force of <s>karma</s>, would take those who follow <s>karma</s> —
if a Lord exists [who is] a follower of <s>karma</s>, what kind of deity is he?' ||18.10||
'All that is offered as oblation dependent on another is offered in ashes;
but what is offered in the household fire (<s>gārhapatya</s>) is the nourishment of self and one's own.' ||18.11||
'Therefore, since we are <s>Ābhīra</s> <s>Vaiśyas</s>, the service of the inviolable cow is our meritorious duty;
if one should regard a deity, she should be regarded as one sheltered in that [cow]!' ||18.12|| [anuṣṭubh]

'Dust, earth, and fodder and the like — the rain has purpose for these; therefore <s>Indra</s> is not the shelter of cows;
but the mountain named <s>Govardhana</s> — he alone is that [shelter]!' ||18.13||
'Cows are our livelihood by nature, and earth-gods (<s>brāhmaṇas</s>) by hereditary duty;
these attain the status of being directly perceptible as shelters — not the sky-dwellers!' ||18.14|| [anuṣṭubh]

 There, he demolishes the alternative view by the opinion of the logicians skilled in sophistry:
'Authoritative testimony and inference are themselves known from direct perception alone;
therefore, direct perception alone would be the proof that crushes all!' ||18.15||
'Therefore, let a great festival be commenced for the cows, <s>brāhmaṇas</s>, and the mountain!
Let this threefold ritual be performed with the oblation-clans [formerly dedicated] to <s>Indra</s>!' ||18.16||
'If a fire-offering is to be performed, let it be offered directed toward Fire;
for he is the shelter of all and is directly experienced!' ||18.17||
'Then let the <s>brāhmaṇas</s> be gratified, with cows given together with fees;
this is your nature — not servitude to ritual injunction!' ||18.18|| [anuṣṭubh]

'The mountain of collyrium — mostly the magnitude of his body — was conquered by his garments [appearing as] twilight-clouds;
his ornamental jewels [shone with] splendors conquering the sun and others;
his crown-ornaments [appeared as] thousands of branches laden with fine flower-burdens — ah!
This mountain-god makes our eyes wondrous!' ||18.31|| [śārdūlavikrīḍita]

He wished to scatter <s>Vraja</s> with winds — [but] he himself was scattered;
he wished to assault with rains — and was himself assaulted; indeed, in <s>Viḍaujā's</s> (<s>Indra's</s>) heart;
he wished to strike with thunderbolts — and attained upon his own head the state of being thunder-struck.
It is fitting that the course of one who causes calamity to the virtuous is reversed!' // [śārdūlavikrīḍita]

Then in the sky, the sun quickly attained a state of clarity;
the directions gained radiance, the earth gained width of path;
and then the aggregate of eyes, ears, and feet of living beings
followed its own function in accordance with its threefold shelter! // [pṛthvī]

But:
Just as <s>Kṛṣṇa</s> had lifted it, so too he set down that mountain;
and it was seen by all the people, but the manner [of how he did it] was not perceived! // [anuṣṭubh]

The cowherd women there, imbued with reverential sentiment,
performed worship of the conqueror of the lord of the gods
with offerings of auspicious substances — curds, unbroken rice, and the like;
and although thus, still placing a hand upon his head, they blessed him;
it is understood: this is that very one who was once experienced in the heart! // [śārdūlavikrīḍita]

The mother wiped his face all over with tears; the father touched his crest-lock;
both, examining his entire body, asked about his well-being;
friends and others massaged his limbs — thus the other <s>Vraja</s>-dwellers,
each having come individually, rendered their service — for he was the life-breath of all! ||18.105|| [śārdūlavikrīḍita]

 "And he (<s>Bṛhaspati</s>) recalls:"
"He who, though bathed, is as if unbathed — indeed he is untouchable;
who, hanging upon a dead body, is a source of fear to the righteous!" // [<s>Gopāla-campū</s> 1.19.5, <s>anuṣṭubh</s>]

His body, though of lovingly dark luster, was invisible from the reddish blaze of wrath;
his two eyes, though destroyers of darkness, blocked sight by the fierceness of their pallid [glow];
his speech too, though possessed of gentleness and other qualities, was terrible from the vehemence of rage —
astonishing those [servants who bore] the semblance of end-of-<s>kalpa</s> clouds, lightning, and thunder! // [śārdūlavikrīḍita]
[15] Then — 'Where is that jackal who hides behind his den?' — thus, with the play of such taunting words, as his gaze was fiercely seeking the one who augmented contempt toward the augmenter of the glorious <s>Nanda</s> family, and was blazing in luster — the lord of all waters (<s>Varuṇa</s>), supporting a great clamorous flow, with his cloth wound around his own neck, his forehead touching the ground, apprehensive of conflict, with joined palms — prostrated at a great distance like an unbroken staff. [16] And from such sight of his diminished pride, all of his (<s>Varuṇa's</s>) lords likewise spread themselves thus. [17] Then, the one of lotus-eyes like fresh dawn-lotuses, pleasing to all, approaching with compassion at the forefront, immediately asked that <s>Varuṇa</s>: 'Where are the feet of my father, of supreme righteous conduct?' [18] And <s>Varuṇa</s>, his composure melting, with joined palms, smeared on a soothing word: 'O Supreme Person! Where your lordship is, there that incomparable, supremely great one exists — and nowhere else! But what punishment shall I administer to these servants, and what instruction for myself — that guidance I beseech!' [19] <s>Kṛṣṇa</s> said with a calmed heart: 'The punishment of these wretches merely shatters the manliness to punish them. But show without delay the lotus of my father's feet!' [20] Then — 'As your lordship commands' — said those of shattered, destitute, and humble disposition! [21] The king of waters made the path from his feet to the vicinity of the chamber where the glorious king of <s>Vraja</s> was resplendent supremely comfortable with the finest cloth, led there the leader of the clan that leads the cowherd clan, and said: 'O <s>Govinda</s>, let your father be taken, O you who love your father!' [BhP 10.28.9] [22] 'I know this very well — so how could I act otherwise? Or how could any defeat come to him from another?' — such was the sentiment. [23] Thus, from the noose of <s>Varuṇa</s> — which was assuming the aspect of a vessel of fear — he who was the liberator even from that [fear] by his own might:

He opened his eyes, and beheld the face
of the glorious one;
from that very sight, he became tearful,
and considered his tear-flowing [experience] as if a dream! // [indravajrā]
[24] And <s>Kṛṣṇa</s>, first touching the soles of his pair of feet and then embracing him, raised him up: 'Father, it is I who have come!' — thus he made him understand. [25] And there, as both were arriving at a state of complete agitation, <s>Hari</s>, his mind contracted by the apprehension that the surrounding attendants of others might mock [them], endeavored to assume an appearance devoid of the distortions of tears and such, both there and within himself, and said: 'Father, let the surroundings be carefully observed!' [26] Then the lord of <s>Vraja</s>, having looked around on all sides, gazed with embarrassment at his son's face, [wondering] 'Where have we come?' [27] And he said: 'Father, let no notion of anything amiss be extended! We shall go to our own <s>Vraja</s> just as we came on the journey. Therefore, let your lotus feet be placed before for guidance.' [28] And there, as he was thus proceeding, <s>Varuṇa</s>, with agitation and full plaintiveness, said:

'Love of no small magnitude always makes everything cooperative;
and if this [love] obtains an appropriate method, then it assumes the state of excellent radiance;
in that [method], the guru herself was that very supreme <s>Vaiṣṇavī</s> power,
worthy of veneration, and the excellent <s>mantra</s> was the bearer of the desired [object] — what further happiness for them beyond this?' ||21.6|| [śārdūlavikrīḍita]

The faces of those [maidens] did not create the confusion of clusters of unfading lotuses there;
for in winter, from the absence of that [blooming],
rather, here [the lotuses] only sank down! ||21.11|| [āryā]

And then:
For a moment they stood in the middle of the water with downcast eyes;
then, as the cold arose, they gazed at the <s>kadamba</s> tree;
whether <s>Hari</s> had previously been present atop a branch with concealed form,
he then uncovered himself for their recognition by them! ||21.12|| [mālinī]

And then:
The boys laughed out loud, and the girls cried out there;
and said: 'But this deed of yours will become a matter of disrespect in <s>Vraja</s>!' ||21.14|| [anuṣṭubh]

 They [the girls] said:
'The prowess of tree-top-going is fit for robbers;
the lowliness of water-going would be that of the robbed!' ||21.18|| [anuṣṭubh]

'The smokes are the bees; the spreading harshness of flames is the gentle touch;
the blazes are clusters of <s>kiṃśuka</s> flowers; the embers in every direction are the cuckoos;
the ashes here are heaps of pollen-blossoms; the fire is Spring in disguise —
alas, alas! Without the dark cloud of <s>Kṛṣṇa</s>, tell us — who is the deliverer of deliverance?' // [śārdūlavikrīḍita]

'I naturally sounded the flute;
therefore, you have come together here;
hence, if other people were to pass by,
I fear — so do not stay here!' // [upajāti]

'Sacrificial food, from the <s>dīkṣā</s> (consecration) through <s>agniṣoma</s>, <s>paśu</s> (animal-offering), <s>aṅga</s> (subsidiary), and <s>homa</s> (oblation) to their conclusion,
is not to be eaten by the wise ritualists;
in the <s>Sautrāmaṇī</s> [sacrifice], never at any time — so say the <s>brāhmaṇas</s>;
but at times other than those two, that [food] is indeed acceptable!' ||22.10|| Thus.

'Oh indeed, greatly fortunate ones! The great burden of your [honor]
has arisen and makes us abashed;
therefore, ask about your welcome!' ||22.23|| Thus.

"The supreme stream of <s>rasa</s> (aesthetic essence),
if it encounters a break in the middle,
from the absence of continuity between the prior and subsequent [portions],
never attains its true nature!" ||23.3|| [<s>gīti</s>]

The face [of <s>Hari</s>] — the germination of abundant enchantment in the host of moon-faced maidens;
the pair of eyes — the whirling of such deer-eyed ones;
the body — the splitter of the composure of the assembly of slender-bodied women — of Śrī <s>Hari</s>:
then the <s>rāsa</s> festival arose, when birth itself sought [its origin]! ||23.4|| [<s>pṛthvī</s>]

The face — enchanter of the moon; the eye — enticer of the lotus-eyed;
the luster — granting the delight of first meeting to the clouds' own radiances;
of the array of <s>Rāmās</s> (beautiful women), adorned with the charm of the lover of <s>Ramā</s> (<s>Lakṣmī</s>):
then the <s>rāsa</s> festival arose, when birth itself sought [its origin]! ||23.5|| [<s>pṛthvī</s>]

 And likewise the latter half:
By poets, moon-hood has been composed upon <s>Kṛṣṇa</s>, and star-hood upon them;
but by the splendors of smiling faces and radiant sports, these [comparisons] are surpassed! ||24.4|| [<s>anuṣṭubh</s>]

Now, what was fit to be told has been told, and some has been made famous;
but what remains untold — that doubly-transitive [matter] —
know it by the method of <s>Pāṇini</s>'s recollection [i.e., by grammatical inference]! ||24.16|| [<s>viyoginī</s>]

Likewise,
love gives birth to wrath — and though vain, the weapon here is the eye;
and that [weapon], though by nature immovable from its place, appears to pierce from afar;
and that piercing, even without a wound, would cause distress to <s>Śrī Hari</s> —
she of such indescribable conduct — who could describe this one? ||25.36|| [<s>mandākrāntā</s>]
That is Śrī <s>Rādhā</s>. These were [the ones] facing him directly.

Above, the moon as a parasol; below, the sandbank — its body strewn with diamond-dust;
the splendid seat, with hundreds of flower-groves on all sides like festive arches;
him — of body resplendent in the row of <s>Vraja</s>-clan-born beauties of divine golden hue,
the abode beloved by the beauty of affection, radiant as the <s>hari</s> gem — my lord of life — I remember! ||25.39|| [<s>sragdharā</s>]

 And when the circle was formed:
The sandbank of <s>Kṛṣṇā</s> (<s>Yamunā</s>), entered by the lover, was then filled with desire;
by that [desire], his mind was [filled] at every step; by that, both his eyes;
by that, the host of women with limbs quivering; by that, the prior and subsequent —
all the junctures — I declare this: what more [can be said]? Even now it does not rest! ||26.4|| [<s>śārdūlavikrīḍita</s>]

