kvacid ahīna-tuhina-marīci-marīci-vīci-rocamāna-kānane paricitāparicita-nānā-vidha-mṛga-pakṣi-vṛkṣa-vallī-vilakṣaṇatā-vilokanāya kṛta-paribhramaṇam;
kvacid api madākula-bhramara-kula-saṅkula-gūḍha-kuḍuṅga-kusuma-caya-cayanāya kampamāna-prāṇa-praṇayinī-janasya prakaṭaṃ cāṭutā-ghaṭanāya tatra balād iva viracita-praveśanābhiniveśam;
kvacid api dhṛṣṭa-ṣaṭpada-daṣṭatā-labdha-kaṣṭa-spaṣṭa-duṣparihara-bāṣpa-lalanāvakalanād uddaṇḍitena sanāla-keli-kharanāla-daṇḍena tad-daṇḍanāya makara-kuṇḍala-tuṇḍa-paryanta-sparśi-roka-koka-nadāyamāna-netra-puṇḍarīkam;
kvacid api lalitavilāsa-valitānana-lalanā-vapur anubhūya jugupsitī-kṛta-sthala-kamala-sthalāvalokanam;
kvacid api śuka-nikarādhigamanīya-ramaṇīya-vāṇī-vilāsa-kalanāya salālana-kalita-kala-kalā-kalāpam;
kvacid api nija-līlā-milad-ullala-bhāva-vilulita-sulalita-mukula-kula-latā-vakalanena vikalatayā vigalita-nayana-jala-leśam;
kvacid api prauḍhatā-samūḍha-gūḍha-garva-parva-kharvaṇāya sadambharambhoru-varūtham anu nirvyūḍha-prarūḍhamāra-samprahāram;
kvacid api mithuna-mithaḥ-keli-kalānukṛti-niyati-valita-praticchāyāvaloka-kautuka-vāhitayā vihita-bahu-hāsam;
kvacid api parasparam aparaspara-sparśa-sukha-parāmarśajāta-nivṛtti-citta-vṛttitayā nimīlad-vilocana-rocamānam;
kvacid api niryantraṇa-ramaṇa-śrama-paratantra-tandrāyām api tad eva ramaṇaṃ bhūyaḥ samanubhūya rāga-sāgarāyamāṇa-jāgaram--

children.translated_text:
Somewhere, wandering through a forest gleaming with the undiminished moon's wave-like rays, for the purpose of beholding the wondrous variety of familiar and unfamiliar animals, birds, trees, and creepers;
somewhere, with a determined effort of entry as if by force, for the purpose of gathering clusters of flowers hidden amidst bee-swarms drunk with intoxication, and for openly displaying flattering charm to the trembling beloved;
somewhere, with lotus-eyes crying out like <s>koka</s>-birds, touching even the tips of the <s>makara</s>-earrings — perceiving the tears of those women, difficult to restrain, [arising from] the clearly experienced distress of being bitten by bold bees — with the upraised lotus-stem-like playful staff for punishing those [bees];
somewhere, having experienced the body of a woman whose face turned with graceful play, [he] made the sight of land-lotuses seem contemptible;
somewhere, a cluster of soft, melodious arts fashioned with caressing skill, for creating the delight of charming speech attainable by flocks of parrots;
somewhere, with a trace of tears flowing from the eyes through the distress of plucking the supremely delicate clusters of bud-vines, shaken by his own playful sport;
somewhere, a fully deployed battle of the sprouting Love-god, carried out along the thigh-ramparts with pomp and splendor, for the purpose of diminishing the hidden nodes of pride overwhelmed by maturity;
somewhere, abundant laughter produced by the curiosity of beholding the reflection turned by fate into an imitation of the amorous art of the couple's mutual play;
somewhere, shining with closing eyes through the mental disposition of withdrawal born from the reflection on the happiness of mutual non-contact;
somewhere, wakefulness becoming an ocean of passion, experiencing that very love-making again even during the drowsiness dependent upon the fatigue of unrestrained love-making —

'When the cowherd women departed from the <s>rāsa</s> to their homes in secret,
then their household members did not even suspect them;
for other ones of identical form, created by <s>Hari's</s> <s>māyā</s>, were placed in each house,
so that it was known by them [the <s>gopīs</s>] that these [doubles] had returned by the road.' ||29.1||
[2] Then, as the glorious <s>Govinda</s> was passing the succession of nights that followed with <s>rāsa</s> sports of various <s>rasas</s> (sentiments), <s>Vṛndā</s> assembled various kinds of assistance, as follows:

 Now, the <s>vipralabdhā</s> (the one deceived/disappointed). And there, the words of the friend to him:
'Your arrival does not merely bestow enjoyment and liberation;
it also manifestly reveals devotion among the well-disposed, O supreme lord!' ||29.23||

'The divine forest avenues, the earth's treasure — the <s>Govardhana</s> mountain;
the <s>rāsa</s> circle shining with various gems, the night bright as the white-rayed moon;
the <s>lāsya</s> (dance) — whose feminine beauty, praised by <s>Lakṣmī</s> and marked [as supreme], bears comparison incomparable, with the resplendent male —
and <s>Rādhikā</s> surpassing all — by whom could all this be described?' ||31.53||

 What more? Here too is this ancient verse of the <s>Purāṇas</s>:
'Just as <s>Rādhā</s> is dear to <s>Viṣṇu</s>, so is her <s>kuṇḍa</s> (pool) dear [to him];
among all the <s>gopīs</s>, she alone is <s>Viṣṇu's</s> supremely beloved!' ||

'Born in <s>Vraja</s>, from [that] birth arose a nature equal to the people of <s>Vraja</s>;
from that, love for <s>Kṛṣṇa</s>; following that, suddenly, a movement toward him;
from that, attachment there; following that, something contrary —
from that, clearly, even that [is] love! O friend, shame [on me]! What indeed shall I do?' ||33.7||

 And he [<s>Nārada</s>] beheld him [<s>Kṛṣṇa</s>] thus — in the excellence of his constant good conduct, like a virtuous lotus;
in the state of always giving happiness, following the dear footsteps, like the <s>Kamaṭha</s> (tortoise <s>avatāra</s>) who had fashioned such bodily splendor;
in the beautification of his fine, excellent anklet-adorned calves, like a victory-banner;
in the praiseworthy named knees and thighs, innately [beautiful], like the pillar of <s>Garutmat</s> (<s>Garuḍa</s>);
in the expansiveness possessing the hump of authority, like his own cliff of <s>Vraja</s>;
in the quality of attachment to auspicious beauty and <s>Prahlāda</s>-like [devotion], like <s>Narasiṃha</s>;
in the navel beautiful [like] the lotus of the world, like a lake — or like <s>Nārāyaṇa</s>;
in the yellow garment drunk by the girdle of excellent qualities, like the rays of a new sun;
in the constant movement of the stomach's breathing, like a <s>pīpala</s> leaf;
in the heart-captivating golden line of Śrī (<s>Lakṣmī's</s> mark) on the chest, like a touchstone-slab for a blue sapphire;
in the heart being a repository of the jewels of good qualities, like a jewel-ocean;
in the arms [serving as] the store of propriety, like the jewel-bolt of <s>Jiṣṇu</s> (<s>Indra</s>/<s>Arjuna</s>);
in the armlets and such having obtained access, like the auspicious [mark] of <s>Kausalyā's</s> womb;
in the state of hands [being] pleasing seal-makers, like the tendrils of a wish-fulfilling creeper;
in the form of the voice of eager anticipation, like the <s>Pāñcajanya</s> conch;
in the state of being a servant of the king of the twice-born star-hosts, like the king of the twice-born (moon);
in the nose adorned with the beauty of conquering the beak of a parrot, like himself indeed;
in the eyes pleasing to devotees of the vow of truth, like fresh divine fish;
in the hair fashioned with the splendor of peacock-crests, like a rain-cloud at the time of the approach of rain;
in the ears adorned with <s>makara</s>-[shaped earrings], like the <s>Makara</s>-bannered one (<s>Kāmadeva</s>);
in the fullness accomplished by the dark form always raining nectar everywhere, again like the nectar-rayed moon;
in the state of being the delight of <s>Rohiṇī</s>, <s>Yaśodā</s>, and <s>Nanda</s>, like the son of <s>Rohiṇī</s> (<s>Balarāma</s>);
in the compact body endowed with the nature of <s>Narasiṃha</s>, again like <s>Narasiṃha</s>;
in the form of ever-new, unprecedented honor, again like the Fish and Tortoise [<s>avatāras</s>];
in the supreme state of shattering the eminence of <s>Bali</s> through skillful determination, like the god <s>Vāmana</s>;
in the state of reproving the bull [marked with] the brand of the one who walks the wrong path, like the son of the <s>Jina</s> (the Buddha);
in the zeal for rescuing and lifting up the lineage submerged in the ocean, again like the blessed tortoise (<s>Kamaṭha</s>);
in the captivating sport of bearing the earth, like the <s>Varāha</s> (Boar) <s>avatāra</s>;
in the state of destroying the wicked <s>kṣatriya</s> race born of <s>Kṛtavīrya</s>, like <s>Bhārgava Rāma</s> (<s>Paraśurāma</s>);
in the [future] subjugation of the <s>Kālayavana</s>, like the son of <s>Viṣṇuyaśas</s> (<s>Kalki</s>);
in the abundant happiness fashioned by the conduct of <s>Lakṣmaṇa</s>, again like Śrī <s>Rāmacandra</s>.

 The sage said:
'Without the moon, the beauty of autumn; without flowers, the exaltation of spring;
without rain-clouds, the splendor of the monsoon; without you, what state for <s>Vraja</s>?' — thus.

Moreover:
'<s>Vraja</s>, with all its limbs and glory, shall go to <s>Kṛṣṇa</s>;
and it shall hold <s>Kṛṣṇa</s> and [all] who follow <s>Kṛṣṇa</s>, together with <s>Rāma</s>!' ||33.89||
'The Śrī <s>Bhāgavata</s> with its twelve <s>skandhas</s> (cantos) sees [it] thus;

husbands, sons, sisters too —
whom we abandoned for your sake, O <s>Dāśārha</s>, those dear ones, hard to abandon, O Lord!' ||[BhP 10.65.11]|| — and so forth;
'She who, abandoning her own people, hard to abandon, and the path of the noble' — and so forth.

'The dice-sport was performed by the Lord
together with the cowherd-folk;
in the form of winning the stakes, the <s>gopīs</s> and their wealth were won;
brought by the cowherds and presented there to <s>Kṛṣṇa</s> himself!' ||

Likewise:
'The first — of moist-limbed, dark, excellent tree-creeper-arm-play, praised in sport;
symmetrically, the other — having obtained a meeting with a branch [used as] a ball-stick through the earnestness of ploughing;
therein, the first — with contracting mind, having prepared a garment for the pervasion of his cluster of limbs;
and moreover, the other — likewise, for the purpose of the act of meeting with him, gazing at the approaching moment!' || (GC 2,2.19) — thus.

'<s>Akrūra</s> and <s>Ānakadundubhi</s> (<s>Vasudeva</s>) — whom he placed at the edges of his own platform;
the groups of citizens sharing [the space]; the daughter of <s>Devaka</s> (<s>Devakī</s>); <s>Nanda</s> and others far away;
moreover, at the gate he placed the elephant <s>Kuvalayāpīḍa</s>; in his own foreground,
the wrestlers through treachery — that king of the <s>Bhojas</s> — who then is not agitated?' ||5.1|| (<s>śārdūlavikrīḍita</s>)

Having slain the elephant and seized the pair of tusks,
the foe of <s>Kaṃsa</s> gave one to his brother —
as if to say: 'One [tusk] fashions the fame of us two;
let it stand in two places, so it may extend in all directions!' ||GC_2,5.8|| (śālinī)

Even while rushing toward the enraged enemy, <s>Hari</s>, bearing perspiration from the exhilaration of <s>rasa</s> upon his un-hostile lotus-face, sports without abandoning his own gentleness;
whereas <s>Rāma</s>, with clearly reddened eyes and face, blazes from that anger —
and that is his nature, for he is the direct fire of <s>Hari's</s> valor! ||GC_2,5.28||

'There too, wise scholars see a great wonder:
east of the <s>Kālīya</s> pool, the <s>kadamba</s> tree is honored,
with a hundred branches, O wide-eyed one, sacred, emitting divine fragrance;
and for twelve months it is lovely, auspicious, and cool;
it blossoms, O wide-eyed one, illuminating the ten directions!' ||
